PetroOS
A Real-Time Computational Infrastructure Platform for Oil & Gas Operations

PetroOS is a proposed software architecture for the continuous observation, modelling, simulation and optimisation of oil and gas assets.

The system is conceived as a computational layer connecting field instrumentation, wells, gathering systems, pipelines, processing facilities and engineering analytics into a unified operational data environment.

Its core architecture separates three functions:

Rust — high-performance industrial telemetry and edge processing
Python — analytics, simulation, optimisation and forecasting
Kotlin — real-time engineering interface and operational visualisation

The central proposition is that oil and gas infrastructure should increasingly be represented as a continuously observed cyber-physical system, in which physical assets, operational telemetry, engineering models and economic variables are computationally related.

This is particularly relevant to an industry in which SCADA, distributed control systems and other operational-technology systems already provide extensive monitoring and control capabilities across geographically distributed assets. NIST explicitly identifies oil and natural-gas pipelines among major applications of SCADA and emphasises the distinctive reliability, safety and cybersecurity requirements of operational technology.

1. Executive Thesis

Modern petroleum operations generate extremely large volumes of time-series information.

A producing asset may simultaneously generate measurements concerning:

Wellhead pressure
Bottom-hole pressure
Flow rates
Temperature
Choke position
Pump performance
Artificial-lift parameters
Vibration
Pipeline pressure
Pipeline flow
Tank levels
Compressor state
Separator conditions
Water production
Gas composition
Equipment health
Energy consumption

Yet the fundamental engineering problem is not simply the acquisition of these measurements.

It is their integration into a coherent computational representation of the physical asset.

PetroOS proposes:

FIELD INSTRUMENTATION
        ↓
REAL-TIME TELEMETRY
        ↓
ASSET STATE
        ↓
DIGITAL MODEL
        ↓
ANALYTICS / SIMULATION
        ↓
OPTIMISATION
        ↓
ENGINEERING DECISION

The objective is therefore to evolve from:

monitoring an oilfield

toward:

computing the state of an oilfield.

2. The Problem

Oil and gas infrastructure is inherently distributed.

A single operational system may span:

WELLS
  ↓
GATHERING SYSTEM
  ↓
SEPARATION
  ↓
PROCESSING
  ↓
STORAGE
  ↓
PIPELINE
  ↓
EXPORT

These assets operate under changing physical conditions and are exposed to:

Reservoir uncertainty
Equipment degradation
Pressure changes
Flow instability
Temperature variation
Water production
Corrosion
Mechanical failure
Energy constraints
Weather
Network disruption

The computational challenge is therefore one of state estimation under uncertainty.

3. PetroOS Architecture
                         PETROOS
                            │
          ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
          ↓                 ↓                 ↓
      INDUSTRIAL         GEOLOGICAL        ECONOMIC
        DATA               DATA              DATA
          │                 │                 │
          └─────────────────┼─────────────────┘
                            ↓
                     DIGITAL ASSET
                            │
              ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
              ↓             ↓             ↓
          SIMULATION     ANALYTICS    OPTIMISATION
              │             │             │
              └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                            ↓
