PetroOS is not intended to replace existing DCS, SCADA or certified safety systems.

Rather, it operates as an integration and intelligence layer above the industrial control environment.

4. Rust — Industrial Telemetry Engine

Rust forms the real-time computational substrate.

SENSORS
   ↓
RTU / PLC / DCS / SCADA
   ↓
RUST EDGE ENGINE
   ↓
VALIDATION
   ↓
NORMALISATION
   ↓
EVENT STREAM
   ↓
PETROOS

The Rust layer provides:

High-throughput telemetry ingestion
Low-latency processing
Sensor validation
Timestamping
Data-quality classification
Local buffering
Protocol adaptation
Event generation
Edge analytics
Real-time streaming

Potential interfaces include appropriate implementations of:

OPC UA
MQTT
Modbus
Industrial Ethernet
REST/gRPC
Vendor-specific APIs

Industrial environments commonly contain heterogeneous protocols and interfaces; NIST documentation specifically discusses technologies including Modbus, HART, PROFIBUS and OPC UA within industrial environments.

5. Python — Petroleum Analytics Engine

Python provides the analytical layer.

Its responsibilities include:

Production forecasting
Well-performance modelling
Reservoir analytics
Artificial-lift optimisation
Equipment health modelling
Anomaly detection
Flow analysis
Energy optimisation
Maintenance forecasting
Scenario simulation
Economic optimisation

The computational architecture becomes:

TELEMETRY
    ↓
STATE ESTIMATION
    ↓
ANALYTICAL MODEL
    ↓
SIMULATION
    ↓
OPTIMISATION
    ↓
RECOMMENDATION

The system should favour transparent engineering models where possible, introducing machine learning when sufficient validated data exists.

6. Kotlin — Operational Engineering Interface

Kotlin provides the real-time operational environment.

The interface is intended for:

Control rooms
Petroleum engineers
Production engineers
Reliability engineers
Asset managers
Field operators
Executive operations centres

A conceptual dashboard:

PETROOS

FIELD: NORTH BASIN

ACTIVE WELLS             184
PRODUCTION               84,200 BOPD
GAS                      212 MMSCFD
WATER                    31,400 BWPD

ACTIVE ALERTS             07

NETWORK                   HEALTHY

FIELD STATUS              ● NORMAL

The objective is high information density without unnecessary visual complexity.

7. Digital Oilfield

The central abstraction is the Digital Oilfield.

FIELD
│
├── WELLS
│   ├── WELLHEAD
│   ├── DOWNHOLE
│   ├── ARTIFICIAL LIFT
│   └── PRODUCTION
│
├── FACILITIES
│   ├── SEPARATORS
│   ├── COMPRESSORS
│   ├── PUMPS
│   └── STORAGE
│
├── PIPELINES
│
├── INSTRUMENTATION
│
└── ENVIRONMENT

Every asset has:

Persistent identity
Location
Current state
Historical state
Telemetry
Maintenance history
Operational constraints
Engineering metadata

The result is a persistent computational representation of the field.

8. Well Telemetry

PetroOS should provide a unified well model.

Example:

WELL-184

PRESSURE
2,841 psi

TEMPERATURE
86°C

OIL
612 BOPD

GAS
1.8 MMSCFD

WATER
148 BWPD

CHOKE
42%

ESP
3,210 RPM

HEALTH
92 / 100

Historical and real-time values can be combined to identify changes in operating behaviour.

9. Production Optimisation

Production can be represented as a constrained optimisation problem.

Conceptually:

MAXIMISE

    Hydrocarbon Recovery

SUBJECT TO

    Reservoir constraints
    Well constraints
    Pressure constraints
    Equipment constraints
    Facility capacity
    Energy consumption
    Water handling
    Safety limits

This allows PetroOS to evaluate scenarios rather than merely report current conditions.

For example:

SCENARIO A
Increase Well 184 choke

Production:
+420 BOPD

Energy:
+2.1%

Pressure margin:
-4%

Risk:
MODERATE

The system presents the engineering consequences rather than automatically changing the physical system.

