12. Equipment Intelligence

PetroOS maintains computational health models for:

Pumps
Compressors
Turbines
Motors
Valves
Separators
Heat exchangers
Compressors
Generators

A conceptual health model:

VIBRATION
   +
TEMPERATURE
   +
PRESSURE
   +
POWER
   +
OPERATING HOURS
   ↓
ASSET HEALTH
   ↓
ANOMALY DETECTION
   ↓
MAINTENANCE FORECAST

The system should distinguish between:

observed failure indicators

and

statistical predictions.

This prevents probabilistic outputs from being represented as engineering certainty.

13. Real-Time Event Architecture

Every significant change becomes an event.

WELL_STARTED
WELL_STOPPED
PRESSURE_CHANGE
FLOW_ANOMALY
HIGH_TEMPERATURE
VIBRATION_WARNING
PUMP_FAILURE
PIPELINE_ALERT
COMMUNICATION_LOSS
MAINTENANCE_REQUIRED
PRODUCTION_CHANGE

Events should be immutable, timestamped and traceable to their source.

This creates a machine-readable history of field operations.

14. Data Quality

PetroOS treats telemetry provenance as a first-class engineering property.

Every measurement should contain:

SOURCE
TIMESTAMP
ASSET
SENSOR
VALUE
UNIT
QUALITY
SEQUENCE

Possible states:

VALID
SUSPECT
INVALID
STALE
DISCONNECTED
SIMULATED

This prevents downstream analytics from treating missing or corrupted data as genuine observations.

15. Edge Computing

PetroOS is deliberately designed for edge-first operation.

FIELD
 │
 ├── LOCAL TELEMETRY
 ├── LOCAL BUFFER
 ├── LOCAL VALIDATION
 └── LOCAL EVENT PROCESSING
          ↓
       NETWORK
          ↓
      CENTRAL SYSTEM

This matters because oil and gas assets may be geographically remote and dependent upon communications infrastructure that cannot be assumed to have continuous availability.

The architecture should therefore degrade gracefully rather than becoming unusable whenever a cloud connection disappears.

16. Cybersecurity

Oil and gas operations constitute operational technology environments in which availability, integrity and safety have materially different requirements from conventional IT systems.

NIST specifically identifies oil and natural gas as major ICS environments and emphasises asset inventory, monitoring and management as components of OT cybersecurity.

PetroOS therefore adopts:

FIELD NETWORK
     │
     ↓
SECURE EDGE
     │
     ↓
DATA DIODES / CONTROLLED GATEWAYS
     │
     ↓
PETROOS

Security capabilities should include:

Mutual authentication
Encryption
Certificate management
Role-based access control
Audit logs
Asset inventory
Network monitoring
Anomaly detection
Secure software updates
Credential management

CISA guidance for ICS/OT monitoring likewise emphasises asset discovery, protocol-aware monitoring and behavioural baselines.

17. Safety Boundary

PetroOS is explicitly not a replacement for safety-instrumented systems, PLCs, DCS or certified emergency-control infrastructure.

The architecture is:

PETROOS
   │
   │ OBSERVES / ANALYSES
   ↓
SCADA / DCS / PLC
   │
   │ CERTIFIED CONTROL
   ↓
PHYSICAL ASSET

PetroOS should not:

Disable safety interlocks
Override emergency shutdown systems
Modify certified safety limits
Directly control critical equipment without an appropriately engineered control layer
Represent predictions as deterministic safety decisions

This separation is fundamental to the system architecture.

